{"id":"9397fba7-77cb-4137-92f6-6ad91a92e7da","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885787,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import copy\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    \\n    # Validate required fields\\n    if not segments or not isinstance(segments, list):\\n        raise ValueError('segments must be a non-empty list')\\n    \\n    n = len(segments)\\n    names = [seg['name'] for seg in segments]\\n    importances = [seg['importance'] for seg in segments]\\n    minima = [seg['minimum'] for seg in segments]\\n    capacities = [seg['capacity'] for seg in segments]\\n    \\n    if any(imp <= 0 for imp in importances):\\n        raise ValueError('importance must be positive')\\n    if any(min_i < 0 for min_i in minima):\\n        raise ValueError('minimum must be non-negative')\\n    if any(cap < 0 for cap in capacities):\\n        raise ValueError('capacity must be non-negative')\\n    if any(min_i > cap for min_i, cap in zip(minima, capacities)):\\n        raise ValueError('minimum exceeds capacity')\\n    \\n    total_min = sum(minima)\\n    total_cap = sum(capacities)\\n    if budget < total_min:\\n        raise ValueError(f'budget {budget} less than total minimum {total_min}')\\n    if budget > total_cap:\\n        raise ValueError(f'budget {budget} exceeds total capacity {total_cap}')\\n    \\n    # Initialize allocations to minima\\n    allocations = list(minima)\\n    remaining = budget - total_min\\n    \\n    # Helper: next position for segment i (current allocation + 1)\\n    # But we need to track current allocation for computing score\\n    current = list(minima)\\n    \\n    while remaining > 0:\\n        best_idx = -1\\n        best_score = -1.0\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            if current[i] >= capacities[i]:\\n                continue  # segment already at capacity\\n            # Score: importance / (current allocation + 1) because next position is current+1\\n            score = importances[i] / (current[i] + 1)\\n            if score > best_score:\\n                best_score = score\\n                best_idx = i\\n            # tie: keep earlier (stable tie-break by original order), so no update on equal\\n        if best_idx == -1:\\n            # Should not happen if budget <= total_cap, but safety\\n            raise ValueError('No more capacity but remaining budget left')\\n        # Allocate one token to best_idx\\n        current[best_idx] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    \\n    # After loop, current holds final allocations\\n    allocations = current\\n    \\n    # Build by_name dict preserving order of segments (though dict order doesn't matter as per schema)\\n    by_name = {names[i]: allocations[i] for i in range(n)}\\n    \\n    total = sum(allocations)\\n    \\n    return {\\n        'allocations': allocations,\\n        'by_name': by_name,\\n        'total': total\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to generate Python code for `solve(case)` that implements the described logic. The function should take a case object (payload) and return a dict with keys: allocations (list of ints), by_name (dict mapping segment names to ints), total (int).\n\nThe logic:\n- Input: case has \"segments\" list, each with name, importance, minimum, capacity. Also \"budget\" (total tokens to allocate).\n- First validate: sum of minima <= budget <= sum of capacities. Otherwise raise ValueError.\n- Initialize each segment to its minimum allocation.\n- Remaining budget = budget - sum(minima).\n- While remaining budget > 0:\n  - For each segment, compute score = importance / (current allocation + 1) ? The description says \"importance divided by its next allocated position\". So if current allocation is a, next position is a+1. So score = importance / (a+1). Or maybe it's importance divided by the next allocated index? Usually in such greedy allocation, we use importance/(current+1). So use that.\n  - Pick segment with maximum score. If tie, choose earlier in original order (stable tie break by original segment order). This is key: we need to preserve order. Since we iterate segments in original list order, we can simply track max score and update if strictly greater, else keep first.\n  - Increment that segment's allocation by 1, decrease remaining budget by 1.\n- After loop, allocate the final allocations.\n- Build allocations list in same order as segments (preserving original segment index order).\n- Build by_name dict: map segment name to final allocation.\n- total = sum of allocations.\n\nWe must not mutate input case. So work on a copy of segments list (or just read values and create new lists/dicts). \nReturn dict with required keys.\n\nEdge cases: If budget equals sum of minima, allocations are just minima; if budget equals sum of capacities, allocations are all capacities.\n\nWe must also handle cases where budget is less than sum minima -> raise ValueError; or budget > sum capacities -> raise ValueError.\n\nThe code must be Python standard library only.\n\nWrite solve(case) function.\n\nNow produce output as JSON with single key \"implementation\" containing the source code as a string (properly escaped). Ensure no extra text.\n\nWe'll construct the Python source as a multi-line string, then JSON-encode it with appropriate escapes.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The dynamicKeysFrom in schema is not relevant to implementation; it's just saying the keys of by_name come from segment names. So we just fill that.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1499,"completion_tokens":1258,"total_tokens":2757,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":541},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":91},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}